Assuming you’re a **U.S.-based online retailer**, I’d shortlist these providers:

| Provider | Best fit | Why consider it for false declines | Watch-outs |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Stripe** | Most standard e-commerce stores; subscriptions; developer-led teams | Adaptive Acceptance, Smart Retries, network tokens, card-account updater, detailed decline data, and integrated Radar fraud controls. ([stripe.com](https://stripe.com/guides/optimizing-authorization-rates?utm_source=openai)) | Fraud rules can still over-block if configured aggressively; pricing and support may be less attractive at very high volume. |
| **Adyen** | Larger or international merchants | Intelligent routing, AI-adjusted authorization messages, smart retries, network-token optimization, and real-time account updater. ([adyen.com](https://www.adyen.com/uplift/optimize/?utm_source=openai)) | Typically a more involved enterprise implementation and sales process. |
| **Braintree** | Stores wanting PayPal/Venmo plus cards, or an easier migration into the PayPal ecosystem | Smart Retries, decline analysis, vaulting, and Account Updater for eligible U.S. merchants. ([developer.paypal.com](https://developer.paypal.com/braintree/articles/control-panel/transactions/declines?utm_source=openai)) | Make sure your implementation distinguishes hard from soft declines; indiscriminate retries can increase fees and hurt approval rates. ([developer.paypal.com](https://developer.paypal.com/braintree/docs/reference/general/processor-responses/authorization-responses?utm_source=openai)) |
| **Shopify Payments** | Stores already on Shopify | Shopify says it automatically routes transactions toward the processor most likely to authorize them; it also provides integrated fraud analysis and configurable AVS/CVV controls. ([help.shopify.com](https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/payments/shopify-payments/transactions/declined-payments?utm_source=openai)) | Availability and eligibility depend on your Shopify account, business type, country, and risk profile. |
| **PayPal Expanded Checkout** | Merchants that want cards plus PayPal, Venmo, Pay Later, and alternative wallets | Supports card processing, wallets, customizable fraud filters, and optional advanced fraud protection. ([paypal.com](https://www.paypal.com/us/business/accept-payments/checkout?utm_source=openai)) | Don’t evaluate it only on the PayPal-branded checkout; test its card-processing performance separately. |
| **Checkout.com** | High-volume, international, or payments-optimized businesses | Intelligent Acceptance adjusts payment data, authentication, routing, network-token use, and retry logic based on issuer and transaction context. ([checkout.com](https://www.checkout.com/products/intelligent-acceptance?utm_source=openai)) | Usually more appropriate for established businesses than small stores seeking a quick plug-in. |

### My practical recommendation

- **Small or midsize store:** Test **Stripe** first; if you use Shopify, test **Shopify Payments** before doing a major migration.
- **Subscriptions or recurring billing:** Compare **Stripe, Adyen, and Braintree**, focusing on account updater, network tokens, and recovery of soft declines.
- **International/high-volume business:** Request proposals from **Adyen and Checkout.com**, with Stripe as the benchmark.
- **You want PayPal/Venmo as an additional payment option:** Consider **Braintree** or **PayPal Expanded Checkout**, but don’t assume adding PayPal will fix card-issuer declines.

### Before switching, verify that the provider is actually the problem

A “declined card” can mean different things:

1. **Issuer decline:** The customer’s bank rejected it. A new processor may not change the result.
2. **Gateway rejection:** Your fraud rules, AVS/CVV settings, velocity limits, or merchant configuration blocked it.
3. **Technical failure:** Bad API payloads, 3-D Secure failures, timeouts, duplicate-payment detection, or processor outages.
4. **Retry problem:** Your system repeatedly retries transactions that should not be retried.

For example, Shopify notes that the customer’s bank—not Shopify—makes many decline decisions, while Braintree distinguishes processor declines from gateway rejections. ([help.shopify.com](https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/checkout-settings/troubleshooting-checkout-payments?utm_source=openai))

Before signing, ask each provider for a **decline breakdown by**:

- issuer response code and network advice code;
- issuer country, card brand, debit/credit, and wallet;
- fraud rejection versus issuer rejection;
- AVS/CVV result;
- 3-D Secure outcome;
- new card versus saved card;
- hard decline versus retryable soft decline;
- checkout/browser/device;
- authorization rate—not merely “successful payment” rate.

### How I’d run the switch

Run a controlled **2–4 week pilot** or route a representative percentage of traffic to the candidate provider. Compare:

- authorization rate;
- checkout completion rate;
- fraud and chargeback rate;
- payment latency and error rate;
- recurring-payment recovery;
- net revenue after processing fees, refunds, disputes, and retry fees;
- support responsiveness.

Do not blindly retry every decline. Stripe recommends using decline advice and limiting retries, and Braintree documents network restrictions and fees associated with excessive retries. ([docs.stripe.com](https://docs.stripe.com/declines/card?locale=it-IT&utm_source=openai))

**Bottom line:** Start with **Stripe or Shopify Payments** for a typical store, **Adyen** for sophisticated/high-volume international processing, and **Braintree** if PayPal/Venmo and card vaulting are important. But first obtain a provider-level decline report—otherwise you may pay for a migration without solving issuer or checkout problems.
